lamic  state  one of their edicts was to basically  wipe out christians in that part of iraq  which was of course mosul um and and the  northern villages leading into kurdistan  the second thing and i've just stressed  that each community has their own  individual challenges um so for instance  in iraq  it's geopolitics but it really also is  climate change um  iraq is one of the most vulnerable  countries in the middle east in terms of  climate change but it's also about  migration mass of vast migration that  these young people there's no industry  there's no incentive for them to stay  so every community has a very different  story to tell the cops in egypt for  instance are extremely discriminated  against it's enshrined within the  egyptian constitution that they cannot  hold offices of high position in the  government or in the um the army which  of course is an arm of the government um  in the gaza strip it's an entirely  different thing there's only 800  christians left there and of course  christianity gaza had been entirely  christian until the 4th century so these  800 remaining people  are basically sandwiched in between  hamas  and the israeli  occupation even though the israelis  withdrew they are still very much  subjugating the people there the 11-day  bombing in may left gaza devastated  again again so each community has has  unique challenges that  are allowing them to remain and indeed  to populate because their numbers are  dwindling drastically but to what extent  were the dictators actually a friend of  the christians because let's take saddam  hussein for example his deputy from  memory tariq aziz was a christian um in  syria and the christians had an okay  time under assad it was when assad was  on the ropes but they started to get  worried and even in egypt the cops were  pretty much i mean correct me if i'm  wrong here but free to worship in peace  until its recent regime changes and only  then do you end up with them christians  in egypt being attacked outside their  churches so i wonder to what to extend  the the regime changes over the last two  decades have ushered in the forces which  washed out the christians  absolutely this was one of the stranger  things that i came across so you're  absolutely right the christians were  protected in iraq under saddam hussein  there was a sort of unofficial deal with  the devil that further voting block  in exchange they would basically be left  alone  the same thing in syria and this is  something that really shocked me  especially as i reported the war in  syria um  when i would go to the christian  communities there they would you know  basically state their absolute love for  bashar al-assad now whether or not this  is a fear that they had to say this or  whether or not it's basically that they  felt we'd rather have assad than have  the devil that might come afterwards in  other words we do not want the black  flag  same with the christians under mubarak  in egypt um and then the ensuing chaos  that led after the egyptian revolution  the muslim brotherhood and as for the  gazans um  they were probably the most closed  community it was the most difficult for  me to penetrate that community even  though i've been working in gaza since  1990 um  and i think basically they  most of the people said that hamas did  not bother them they were more what was  more disturbing for them was their  inability to leave gaza to join their  brethren in bethlehem for christmas or  easter and the israeli authorities  prevented them from doing that but  absolutely the link between christians  and dictators is very strong and very  very strange  all right and do you think that  christians um  you know who would leave any of these  countries could come to britain and  claim under the human definition a  reasonable fear of persecution because  from what you'd say in your book  it's a pretty brave guy who keeps going  to church right now in some of these  areas where you become 